{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently above realized prices across most horizons; apply a downward level shift (stronger in downtrends).",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when recent history is falling, the model extrapolates too gently and stays too high; increase sensitivity to negative momentum (steepen the decline path).",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: the overprediction grows with forecast horizon (errors widen after ~10–15 days); use a horizon-increasing downward correction (e.g., linear or piecewise ramp).",
    "Overestimation of rebounds: model frequently predicts mid-horizon recoveries/mean reversion that do not materialize; dampen mean-reversion strength and cap rebound magnitude after drawdowns.",
    "Volatility asymmetry: downside moves are larger/faster than the model implies; incorporate a negative-skew adjustment (larger corrections when recent returns are negative)."
  ]
}
